# #1221 pinned a DEVIATION here: a constructor alias was resolved where it was READ, so a closure
# capturing one saw the binding in effect where the closure body was CHECKED, not where it was
# called. That diverged from Python and had a `constructor-alias-eager-capture` entry in
# docs/deviations.yaml.
#
# #1248 retires the alias, which deletes the deviation rather than fixing it: there is no longer a
# construct that behaves this way. Both bindings below are refused where they are written.
#
# The replacement idiom is an annotated function-typed variable holding a lambda, which is a real
# delegate with a real runtime value and captures the way Python does — pinned in
# constructor_reference_annotated_rebind.
def main():
    f = float
    g: (str) -> float = lambda s: f(s)

    h = int
    print(g("42"))
    print(h)
